  <h2 id="tracks">The 7 real tracks, ranked by scalability</h2>

  <p>
    The tracks below are ordered by real growth headroom and ownership potential, not by conventional prestige or which one has the hardest entrance exam.
    Tech and data lead because they carry the fewest hard entry gates alongside one of the strongest ceilings. Government and defence sit further down not because they are weak, but because their upside is structurally capped by promotion boards rather than market demand.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>The recurring pattern worth noticing:</strong> almost every high-ceiling track on this list scales through ownership eventually, an own tech product, an own design studio, an own CA or law practice, an own agency, not a bigger title inside someone else’s company. Keep that in mind as you compare timelines below.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="tech">Tech, data, and product roles: the highest realistic ceiling</h2>

  <p>
    This is the strongest option on this entire list if your excluded reason was disliking the JEE grind rather than disliking logical or technical work itself.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    {techCards.map((item) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="feature-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      "No engineering degree needed for tech" is a half-truth. You do not need a B.Tech or a JEE rank to get hired as a developer or data analyst anymore. You absolutely need a working portfolio, because nobody hires "I like computers" on its own.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="design">Design, architecture, and creative-tech</h2>

  <p>
    If the daily work you actually want involves building, shaping, or visualising something concrete, this track deserves a serious look, not a backup-option glance.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    {designCards.map((item) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="feature-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>Scalable-ownership path:</strong> a strong designer or architect who builds a client base and a visible portfolio can eventually run an own studio, setting pricing, choosing projects, and hiring as the practice grows, instead of staying capped at one salary forever.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="business">Business, finance, and CA/CS/CMA: open at the door, harder further in</h2>

  <p>
    This group looks like one track from the outside. It is really several, with very different real difficulty once you look past the undergraduate entry stage.
  </p>

